        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Painting a concrete floor is a prep job with a little painting at the end. Test for moisture, remove every trace of grease and old sealer, give the surface some tooth by etching or grinding, fill the cracks, prime when the label says so, and then roll two thin coats in mild weather. Done that way, a painted floor can look good for years.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Done the fast way, it usually peels within a season, and stripping failed paint is much harder than prepping bare concrete was. If your moisture test fails or you park on the floor daily, take that as a sign to consider a stain or a two-part epoxy instead.
          </p>
